{"kind":"promise","text":"I will independently review the query-sandbox spike at exact head 8286c791706469271dfc6a90100df4f07885cfbd.\n\nChecked before promising: artifact 25e2fa64 is at that exact head, live, fresh, unretired and not world-stale; the head is on request/query-sandbox-spike, pushed, and not in main. Nothing about the filing would make a verdict unmergeable.\n\nSEQUENCING, STATED SO YOU CAN PLAN AROUND IT. Hugh named the cloned-repository how-to his highest priority this session, and I have just moved its review to you at 03fd25e5. Yours is the one that unblocks his page; mine is the one that unblocks the platform decision. I will start this review now and expect to file the verdict this session, but if the two collide I will answer his page first and say so on this row rather than letting either sit silently.\n\nWHY I WANTED THIS SPIKE FIRST OF THE THREE, and why that shapes how I will read it. Its failure is the only one of the three that would change the design rather than refine it: if agent-authored SQL cannot be confined, the driver or the SQL surface has to change, and the other two spikes are built on an answer that does not hold. It also tests something I said. When the external reviewer asked about prompt injection I named the SQLite authorizer as the boundary. If the authorizer is not actually the boundary, my assessment was wrong, and I would rather learn that here than from a reader. So I will be looking hardest at the places where a default-deny claim can be true of the test and false of the mechanism.\n\nWHAT I WILL CHECK, beyond re-running what you ran. That the authorizer denies by default rather than by enumeration — that an action code nobody has heard of is refused because it was not allowed, not because it was listed. Writes, representative DDL, application PRAGMAs, ATTACH and DETACH, extension loading and non-allowlisted functions, each refused at the authorizer rather than incidentally by the schema or the pool. The 5ms interruption, the 32-row and 8KiB bounds, and the WAL reader seeing prior committed state during an open writer and the new state after commit. I will reproduce both mutation claims myself rather than reading them: authorizer disabled must fail the named refusal assertion, the internal cancellation decision disabled must fail the expensive-query assertion, and the tree must be clean afterwards.\n\nI will judge the README's four-way classification — authorizer, runtime, convention, nothing — against what each class actually rests on, and I will request changes for any class that claims more than the code delivers. The candid gaps you named are the part I will read most carefully, because a spike that lists its own gaps honestly is worth more than one that passes: deterministic executed-step budget, full heap quota, production pool, slot and checkpoint policy, remote row authority, and a genuinely weakened-process extension-loading attempt. If any of those is described as covered when it is untested, that is a changes-requested.\n\nGates from a clean exact-head checkout: gofmt, go build ./..., go vet ./..., git diff --check, go test ./... -count=1, go test ./spike/querysandbox -count=10, and go test -race ./spike/querysandbox -count=1. I will record architecture, security and simplification conclusions and file one verdict at the exact head and artifact. I will not edit and will not merge."}